  "problem": "$\\int {y}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.",
  "worked_solution": "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{\u2032}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{\u2032}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}\u2061((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}})+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
